# Form — the wrapper that puts an action's own states around a set of fields. It
# takes the ACTION by reference, so the three things every form has to render are
# read off the one declaration instead of being tracked in cells the host has to
# keep in step:
#
#     use Form(request):
#         form "Send request" -> request(name, email, note):
#             use TextField("Name", "Ada Lovelace", true, "", "", name)
#             use TextField("Email", "you@example.com", true, "", "", email)
#             use TextAreaField("What for?", "One or two sentences.", true, "", "", note)
#
# `pending(a)` while it is in flight, `failed(a)` for the message from whichever
# `check` refused it, and nothing at all the rest of the time. A form built this
# way has no `submitting` flag to reset, no `error` cell to clear, and no way for
# the spinner and the request to disagree.
#
# ── WHY `SubmitButton` IS GONE ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This file used to ship `SubmitButton(label, a: action)`, which lowered to
# `button "{label}" -> a` — an action reference invoked with NO arguments — and a
# note saying that made it no-argument-only. That note was true and the component
# was still wrong, and a real site found out how wrong: every field in a form is a
# `@client` cell, a server-placed action cannot read client state, so the values
# have to arrive as ARGUMENTS. `use SubmitButton("Send", request)` was rejected
# with *action "request" takes 6 argument(s), got 0* — not in an unusual form, in
# the only kind of form there is. There is no varargs and no way to forward a call
# site's expressions through an `action` reference, so no component can ever be
# the submit control of a form that carries data. A component named `SubmitButton`
# that cannot submit a form is a trap in the shape of a convenience, so it is not
# here any more.
#
# THE SUBMIT CONTROL OF A REAL FORM IS THE LANGUAGE'S `form` NODE, written in this
# component's slot: `form "Send" -> request(a, b, c):` takes the arguments because
# it is at the call site, where the arguments are. That is not a workaround for a
# missing facet — it is the node whose entire job this is, and it brings the
# browser's own submit behaviour (Enter to send, a real `<button type="submit">`)
# with it. What `Form` adds is the half a bare `form` node has no opinion about:
# the pending state, the failure message, and the styling of the button the node
# renders for itself. The `.fa-form` rules below are why a form written this way
# looks like this library rather than like an unstyled browser default — the site
# that hit the bug had to write them itself.
#
# The two pieces that WERE reusable survive under names that do not lie:
#
#   * `FormActions()` — the strip under the fields where submit controls sit. A
#     slot, because what goes in it is a `button` with the caller's arguments, or
#     a cancel next to a save, or nothing at all.
#   * `ActionButton(label, a: action)` — a button for an action that genuinely
#     takes no arguments (`logout`, `markAllRead`, `retry`), with that action's own
#     spinner beside it. Same body the old component had; the arity check still
#     refuses an action with parameters, and now the name says so first.
#
# The error is rendered by `FieldError`, the same component a single field's error
# line uses, so a check that failed on a field and a check that failed on the form
# look the same — because they are the same kind of thing.
import "fieldmessage.fct"
import "../ui/spinner.fct"
app FacetForms:
    css:
        [data-fa-mount] .x-form { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 4px;
                 border: none; background: none; padding: 0; }
        # The `form` node's own element. It stacks its children the way this
        # library stacks fields, and its submit button — which the node renders
        # itself, after the children, and which no call site can put a class on —
        # is styled here to match the one in `.x-form-actions`. Without this rule a
        # form whose submit is the node (which is every form that carries data)
        # gets the browser's default button under this library's fields.
        [data-fa-mount] .x-form .fa-form { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 4px;
                 align-items: stretch; }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-form .fa-form > button[type="submit"] { align-self: flex-end;
                 margin-top: 8px; }
        # The action row is a strip under the fields, not a field: it is where the
        # spinner and the button sit together so that "working" appears next to the
        # thing that was clicked rather than at the top of the form.
        [data-fa-mount] .x-form-actions { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 12px;
                     justify-content: flex-end; padding-top: 8px; border: none;
                     background: none; }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-form-actions button,
        [data-fa-mount] .x-form .fa-form > button[type="submit"] {
                                 background: var(--fa-accent); color: #fff; border: none;
                                 font-weight: 700; font-size: 15px; padding: 10px 20px;
                                 border-radius: 9999px; cursor: pointer; }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-form-actions button:hover,
        [data-fa-mount] .x-form .fa-form > button[type="submit"]:hover { filter: brightness(1.08); }
    component Form(a: action):
        box class "x-form":
            slot
            # The actions row is inside the `if`, not around it: an always-rendered
            # row with padding and nothing in it is 8px of unexplained air under
            # every form that is not currently submitting.
            if pending(a):
                row class "x-form-actions":
                    use Spinner("")
            use FieldError(failed(a))
    component FormActions():
        row class "x-form-actions":
            slot
    component ActionButton(label: text, a: action):
        row class "x-form-actions":
            if pending(a):
                use Spinner("")
            button "{label}" -> a
